From 1045000000000000000000000000000000001045 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: y700 workspace <304558835+bobsixsixsix-boop@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 03:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH y8 1045] PCI: qcom + baldur DT: L1SS exit timing and an optional LTR ceiling for the WCN7860 link

NOT BUILT, NOT TESTED on the tablet (written on the Win11 side, 2026-10-16;
research in linux/power/PCIE-L1SS.md; git apply --check passes on
v7.3-rc4 + 0049 + 0056 + the tb323fu dts, hunks offset by 6 lines). Apply
after 1041/1042 on the v7.3-rc4 + tb323fu 0001-0130 tree. Two independent
parts; part 1 is recommended regardless, part 2 is a contingency that stays
off unless the setpci experiment in tools/y700-l1ssbench.sh shows a gain:

1. arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/baldur-wifi.dtsi: t-power-on-us = <80> on the
   Root Port node. The Root Port's L1 PM Substates capability reports
   T_POWER_ON = 0, so aspm.c computes LTR_L1.2_THRESHOLD and T_PwrOn as if
   the PHY/REFCLK came back instantly on L1.2 exit; Qualcomm describes the
   result as "improper L1.2 exit behavior" with AER reports ("PCI: qcom:
   Program correct T_POWER_ON value for L1.2 exit timing", 2025-11, where
   80 us = scale 1 value 8 was hardcoded for all their targets). The
   property is consumed by pcie-qcom since v7.3 (e9769bb17269 "PCI: qcom:
   Program T_POWER_ON"); no upstream DT sets it yet. Zero effect until the
   link really uses L1.2, which today it almost never does (31 entries in
   4 h vs 1.63 M L1.1 entries).

2. drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c: module parameter
   pcie_qcom.ltr_max_latency_us (default 0 = no change). When set, the
   post-enumeration bus walk programs that value into the LTR Max Snoop /
   Max No-Snoop Latency registers of every endpoint whose registers read 0,
   with LTR disabled around the write (PCIe r6.0 sec 6.18) and before
   pci_enable_link_state_locked() turns the L1 substates on. The ordering
   holds because pci_walk_bus() visits the Root Port first and that visit
   is a no-op for ASPM (pcie_aspm_get_link() has no upstream bridge for a
   Root Port, aspm.c), so the link's L1SS is only enabled on the endpoint's
   visit, after this write. On resume post_init runs again, before the
   endpoint's own pci_restore_state(): after the PERST cycle the registers
   read 0 and L1SS is still disabled, so the write simply repeats (the
   later restore writes the same value); if the config space survived they
   read non-zero and nothing is touched. Nothing on a devicetree platform programs these
   registers (x86 BIOS does); a firmware that reads 0 as "I may request
   0 ns" then never lets the Downstream Port pick L1.2 over L1.1
   (precedent: Kioxia NVMe, PCI/ASPM "Update LTR threshold based upon
   reported max latencies" thread, 2022). For the WCN7860 this is the
   least likely of the three explanations in PCIE-L1SS.md: the link did
   enter L1.2 31 times with these registers at 0, and Android's pci-msm
   leaves them at 0 as well while its DRV state machine relies on L1.2.
   The more likely story is that the 4 h count was taken with firmware
   power save off (before 1041) and that host-side link activity (134 L1
   exits/s) keeps the firmware's activity-based LTR at its "active" value.
   Whether the ceiling matters at all is exactly what the setpci
   experiment in tools/y700-l1ssbench.sh (state 7, LTR_US=3000) decides;
   only turn this parameter on if that experiment shows a measurable gain.
   Build note: the code reads pdev->ltr_path, which include/linux/pci.h
   declares under #ifdef CONFIG_PCIEASPM, so this half needs PCIEASPM=y
   (config-t49-y9 has it); it is not upstreamable as written.

Expected saving is small either way: the whole associated-idle Wi-Fi+PCIe
residual is about 0.06 W and L1.1 -> L1.2 only removes the PHYs' common
mode keepers (estimate <= 0.01-0.02 W, below the +-0.03 W battery noise).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/baldur-wifi.dtsi | 11 ++++
 drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c    | 71 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 82 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/baldur-wifi.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/baldur-wifi.dtsi
index 01f07af..e0115c2 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/baldur-wifi.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/baldur-wifi.dtsi
@@ -191,6 +191,17 @@
 	wake-gpios = <&tlmm 104 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
 	reset-gpios = <&tlmm 102 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
 
+	/*
+	 * The Root Port's L1SS capability reports T_POWER_ON = 0, so aspm.c
+	 * derives LTR_L1.2_THRESHOLD and T_PwrOn as if the PHY and REFCLK
+	 * came back instantly on L1.2 exit. 80 us is what Qualcomm programs
+	 * on all its targets ("PCI: qcom: Program correct T_POWER_ON value
+	 * for L1.2 exit timing", 2025-11: scale 1, value 8); pcie-qcom reads
+	 * it from the Root Port node since v7.3 (e9769bb17269). It changes
+	 * nothing until the link really uses L1.2.
+	 */
+	t-power-on-us = <80>;
+
 	wifi@0 {
 		compatible = "pci17cb,110e";
 		reg = <0x10000 0x0 0x0 0x0 0x0>;
diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
index b58a607..5315348 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
@@ -193,6 +193,20 @@
 #define QCOM_PCIE_LINK_SPEED_TO_BW(speed) \
 		Mbps_to_icc(PCIE_SPEED2MBS_ENC(pcie_get_link_speed(speed)))
 
+/*
+ * Nothing on a devicetree platform programs an Endpoint's LTR Max Snoop /
+ * Max No-Snoop Latency registers (on x86 the BIOS does, typically 1-3 ms).
+ * They power up as 0, and at least one firmware family then reports an LTR
+ * of 0, "tolerates no delay" (Kioxia NVMe, see the PCI/ASPM "Update LTR
+ * threshold based upon reported max latencies" thread, 2022), so the
+ * Downstream Port never chooses L1.2 over L1.1. Let the platform supply the
+ * ceiling the BIOS would have supplied. 0 (default) changes nothing.
+ */
+static u32 ltr_max_latency_us;
+module_param(ltr_max_latency_us, uint, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(ltr_max_latency_us,
+		 "Program this LTR Max Snoop/No-Snoop Latency (us) into endpoints whose LTR registers read 0; 0 = leave them (default)");
+
 struct qcom_pcie_resources_1_0_0 {
 	struct clk_bulk_data *clks;
 	int num_clks;
@@ -1107,6 +1121,59 @@ static int qcom_pcie_post_init_2_7_0(struct qcom_pcie *pcie)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+static void qcom_pcie_set_ltr_max_latency(struct pci_dev *pdev, u32 lat_us)
+{
+	u64 ns = (u64)lat_us * 1000;
+	u32 cur, val = 0, scale;
+	u16 ltr;
+
+	if (pci_pcie_type(pdev) != PCI_EXP_TYPE_ENDPOINT)
+		return;
+
+	ltr = pci_find_ext_capability(pdev, PCI_EXT_CAP_ID_LTR);
+	if (!ltr)
+		return;
+
+	/* Some devices only support dword access to LTR (see aspm.c) */
+	pci_read_config_dword(pdev, ltr + PCI_LTR_MAX_SNOOP_LAT, &cur);
+	if (cur) {
+		pci_dbg(pdev, "LTR max latencies already %#x, leaving them\n", cur);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	/* LatencyScale n = 2^(5n) ns, LatencyValue is 10 bits; round up */
+	for (scale = 0; scale < 6; scale++) {
+		u64 unit = 1ULL << (5 * scale);
+
+		if (ns <= (u64)PCI_LTR_VALUE_MASK * unit) {
+			val = DIV_ROUND_UP_ULL(ns, unit);
+			break;
+		}
+	}
+	if (scale == 6) {
+		scale = 5;
+		val = PCI_LTR_VALUE_MASK;
+	}
+	val |= scale << PCI_LTR_SCALE_SHIFT;
+	val |= val << 16;			/* No-Snoop: the same */
+
+	/*
+	 * PCIe r6.0 sec 6.18: program the ceilings before LTR is enabled.
+	 * pci_configure_ltr() already enabled it during enumeration, but the
+	 * L1 substates are not enabled yet at this point (the devicetree
+	 * default is L0s/L1 only; pci_enable_link_state_locked() below turns
+	 * them on), so toggling LTR off around the write is legal.
+	 */
+	pcie_capability_clear_word(pdev, PCI_EXP_DEVCTL2, PCI_EXP_DEVCTL2_LTR_EN);
+	pci_write_config_dword(pdev, ltr + PCI_LTR_MAX_SNOOP_LAT, val);
+	if (pdev->ltr_path)
+		pcie_capability_set_word(pdev, PCI_EXP_DEVCTL2,
+					 PCI_EXP_DEVCTL2_LTR_EN);
+
+	pci_info(pdev, "LTR Max Snoop/No-Snoop Latency set to %u us (%#x)\n",
+		 lat_us, val);
+}
+
 static int qcom_pcie_enable_aspm(struct pci_dev *pdev, void *userdata)
 {
 	/*
@@ -1114,6 +1181,10 @@ static int qcom_pcie_enable_aspm(struct pci_dev *pdev, void *userdata)
 	 * substates.
 	 */
 	pci_set_power_state_locked(pdev, PCI_D0);
+
+	if (ltr_max_latency_us)
+		qcom_pcie_set_ltr_max_latency(pdev, ltr_max_latency_us);
+
 	pci_enable_link_state_locked(pdev, PCIE_LINK_STATE_ALL);
 
 	return 0;
-- 
2.43.0
